# Central exceptions for the image scan in .github/workflows/build-dev-images.yml
# (`trivy image`, gate policy: --ignore-unfixed --severity HIGH,CRITICAL).
#
# Every entry needs two comment lines above it, and an entry without them is a
# review comment, not a merge:
#
#   # why: <what makes this finding not apply to these images>
#   # until: YYYY-MM-DD -- <what happens on that date>
#   CVE-0000-00000
#
# A reason may wrap onto indented continuation lines; what may not happen is an
# entry without both markers.
#
# The date is a convention, not a mechanism: trivy's plain .trivyignore has no
# expiry field, so nothing enforces it — that is precisely why it has to be
# written down and re-read. An entry past its date is either renewed with a
# fresh reason or deleted; "it was here when I got here" is not a reason.
#
# Scope: these images are DEVELOPMENT and CI images. That is an argument for
# some exceptions (a CVE reachable only through a service nothing here runs) and
# for none at all in the direction people usually reach for — a dev image that
# eleven repos boot on every push is not an image where an unaudited exception
# is cheap.
#
# Second thing this format cannot do: scope an entry to a path. Every ID below
# is suppressed image-wide, so a line excused because upstream Joomla or
# upstream buildkit ships a vulnerable copy ALSO hides the same CVE if it ever
# appears in a tree this repo controls (images/lib/oxlint, images/lib/oxfmt).
# Where that overlap is real it is named in the entry. The compensating check is
# `npm audit` on those two lockfiles, which trivy is not the only reader of.
#
# What is deliberately NOT in this file: everything under
# /usr/lib/node_modules/npm. That tree is npm's own bundled dependencies
# (tar, glob, minimatch, sigstore, cross-spawn), it was the bulk of the
# findings, and it is fixed by installing a newer npm over the one NodeSource's
# nodejs 20 package ships — see NPM_VERSION in images/lib/install-dev-tools.sh.
# An exception there would have been an exception for a thing we could fix.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# npm's own bundled brace-expansion
#
# /usr/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion. The one finding in
# npm's tree that installing a newer npm does not move: npm 11.19.0 and npm
# 12.0.2 both bundle brace-expansion 5.0.7, verified by reading the version out
# of both. 12.0.2 is the current npm, so there is no release carrying the 5.0.8
# fix to install over it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# why: memory exhaustion in brace-expansion's expand(); in this tree only npm's
#   own CLI calls it, on the glob and workspace patterns of whatever project the
#   developer runs npm in. Nothing the images serve reaches it, and no npm
#   release bundles the fix yet. NOTE: this ID is suppressed image-wide, and
#   images/lib/oxlint resolves brace-expansion 5.0.9 — a regression there would
#   be hidden from trivy and would have to be caught by `npm audit`.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- re-read the bundled version (`node -p
#   'require("/usr/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion/package.json").version'`)
#   and delete this entry as soon as an npm release ships >= 5.0.8.
CVE-2026-14257

# why: the follow-up to CVE-2026-14257 — unbounded intermediate arrays that
#   bypass the mitigation shipped for it, so npm's bundled 5.0.7 is affected
#   again. Same reachability as above: only npm's own CLI calls expand(), on
#   the developer's glob and workspace patterns. Still nothing to install over
#   it — brace-expansion 5.0.9 is released, but npm 12.0.2 is the current npm
#   and it bundles 5.0.7 (both checked 2026-08-03). This ID also covers
#   brace-expansion 1.1.11 in buildkit's jshint@2.13.4 -> minimatch@3.0.8 tree,
#   the same chain already excused under CVE-2026-13149.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- as for CVE-2026-14257: re-read the bundled version and
#   drop both entries once an npm release ships >= 5.0.9.
CVE-2026-69152

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CiviCRM core's bundled frontend libraries
#
# core/bower_components/* in every flavor. These are CiviCRM's own vendored
# assets, byte-identical to what every CiviCRM release and every production
# site ships; the images install the official CiviCRM build and do not rewrite
# it. Replacing them here would mean shipping a CiviCRM that behaves
# differently from the one the extension under test runs against in production
# — which is the one thing these images exist to avoid.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# why: jquery 1.12.4 is the jQuery CiviCRM itself serves on every page
#   (CRM/Core/Resources/Common.php loads bower_components/jquery/dist/
#   jquery.min.js). The advisory needs the page to make a cross-origin ajax
#   request without `dataType` to a host an attacker controls, so the result is
#   run through globalEval; CiviCRM's own ajax is same-origin. Not fixable
#   downstream: the jQuery major bump is a CiviCRM core decision.
# until: 2027-02-01 -- recheck whether CiviCRM core has moved bower_components/
#   jquery off 1.x; if it has, this entry goes and the images get it for free.
NSWG-ECO-328

# why: js-yaml 3.13.1 in core/bower_components is loaded by exactly one screen,
#   the APIv4 Explorer (CRM/Api4/Page/Api4Explorer.php). The quadratic-time
#   parse needs a crafted merge-key chain in the YAML being parsed — which on
#   that screen is the document the logged-in developer typed into it. Same
#   core-asset argument as jquery above.
# until: 2027-02-01 -- recheck against CiviCRM core's bower_components.
CVE-2026-59869

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# civicrm-buildkit's own node_modules (buildkit-based flavors only)
#
# /home/buildkit/buildkit/node_modules is created by upstream's
# bin/civi-download-tools from civicrm-buildkit's committed package-lock.json:
# jshint, jasmine-core, watch, civicrm-cv, and the optional karma stack. The
# parent chains below are `npm ls` output from the built image, not a guess.
#
# Nothing the images run loads any of it — the entrypoint serves the baked site
# through Apache/PHP, and no /usr/local/bin/ck* tool references the tree. It is
# reached only when a developer invokes buildkit's JS test tooling by hand, on
# a source tree they mounted themselves.
#
# Not fixable here either: pinning our own versions over the clone would mean
# the buildkit flavor no longer reproduces a real civicrm-buildkit install,
# which is the only reason that flavor exists. `npm audit fix` in the clone at
# build time was considered and rejected for the same reason, plus it would
# resolve to whatever is current on each monthly rebuild — the drift this
# repo pins everything else against.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# why: braces 3.0.2 via karma@6.4.4 -> chokidar@3.5.2. Memory exhaustion needs
#   imbalanced braces in a watch pattern, i.e. a pattern the developer running
#   karma wrote. Reached only by hand-run karma, never by the served site.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild; upstream bumping karma
#   or its lockfile drops this without any change here.
CVE-2024-4068

# why: cross-spawn 4.0.2 via civicrm-cv@0.1.2 -> child-process-promise@2.2.1.
#   The ReDoS is in parsing the command string, and the command strings in that
#   chain come from buildkit's own scripts, not from request input.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild.
CVE-2024-21538

# why: form-data 2.3.3 via karma-phantomjs-launcher@1.0.4 ->
#   phantomjs-prebuilt@2.1.16 -> request@2.88.0. phantomjs-prebuilt is an
#   OPTIONAL dependency of an abandoned browser launcher; nothing in these
#   images posts multipart bodies through it. Parameter pollution via the
#   predictable boundary requires an application that sends attacker-chosen
#   field values to a remote endpoint — there is no such caller here.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild; upstream dropping
#   karma-phantomjs-launcher removes the whole chain.
CVE-2025-7783

# why: form-data 2.3.3, same abandoned phantomjs chain as above. CRLF injection
#   needs attacker-controlled field names or filenames passed to
#   FormData#append; nothing in the image calls it at all.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild.
CVE-2026-12143

# why: lodash 4.17.23 via jshint@2.13.4 and karma@6.4.4. The RCE is in
#   _.template with attacker-controlled options.imports KEY names; jshint and
#   karma do not expose _.template to any input, and neither is on any request
#   path in these images.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild; lodash 4.18.0 is
#   released, so this clears as soon as upstream's lockfile moves.
CVE-2026-4800

# why: brace-expansion 1.1.11 via jshint@2.13.4 -> minimatch@3.0.8. Exponential
#   expand() on a crafted pattern, and the patterns are the file globs a
#   developer hands jshint.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild.
CVE-2026-13149

# why: minimatch 3.0.8 via jshint@2.13.4 and karma@6.4.4 (both directly and
#   through glob@7.x). ReDoS on a crafted glob pattern — again the developer's
#   own pattern, in tooling that never sees a request. NOTE: suppressed
#   image-wide; images/lib/oxlint resolves minimatch 10.2.6, which is fixed, and
#   a regression there would only be caught by `npm audit`.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild.
CVE-2026-26996

# why: minimatch 3.0.8, same two parents. Unbounded recursive backtracking on
#   non-adjacent ** segments in a developer-supplied pattern. Same image-wide
#   suppression caveat as CVE-2026-26996.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild.
CVE-2026-27903

# why: minimatch 3.0.8, same two parents. Catastrophic backtracking from nested
#   *() extglobs in a developer-supplied pattern. Same image-wide suppression
#   caveat as CVE-2026-26996.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild.
CVE-2026-27904

# why: guzzle 7.15.1 in the vendor/ trees of seven buildkit CLI tools
#   (tools/civici, releaser, zipdiff, …). Host-check bypass needs an attacker
#   controlling the request URL of a running HTTP client; these are hand-run
#   release/maintenance scripts talking to endpoints their invoker chose, and
#   nothing the image serves loads them. Same not-ours-to-pin reasoning as the
#   node_modules block above. NOTE: suppressed image-wide; extension repos lock
#   their own guzzle and their osv gate still catches this ID.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild; upstream re-locking
#   guzzle >= 7.15.2 drops this.
CVE-2026-69246

# why: ip-address 10.2.0 in buildkit's node tree. The octet-decoding mismatch
#   matters when untrusted input reaches an IP allow/deny decision; the only
#   consumers here are hand-run dev tooling that never takes network input.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild.
CVE-2026-69192

# why: socket.io-parser 4.2.6 via karma's socket.io stack. Memory exhaustion
#   needs an attacker speaking Socket.IO to a running server — karma's dev
#   server only runs when a developer starts it by hand, bound for a local test
#   browser; the served site never opens a socket.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after a buildkit rebuild; upstream bumping karma
#   or its lockfile drops this.
CVE-2026-69185

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Drupal's package_manager test FIXTURE (drupal11 flavors)
#
# core/modules/package_manager/tests/fixtures/fake_site/vendor/composer/
# installed.json declares drupal/core, drupal/core-dev and
# drupal/core-recommended at 9.8.0. There is no Drupal 9.8.0 in the image: that
# vendor/ directory contains no PHP file other than composer's own
# installed.php, and the Drupal the site actually runs is 11.4.4
# (Drupal::VERSION). Trivy is reading a hand-written fixture manifest that
# describes an imaginary site, which is exactly what package_manager's tests
# need it for. Deleting the fixture would break Drupal core's own test suite.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# why: no drupal/core 9.8.0 code exists in the image — the finding comes from
#   a package_manager test fixture's installed.json, whose vendor/drupal/core
#   directory is empty of code. The deserialization gadget chain has nothing to
#   be in.
# until: 2027-02-01 -- recheck that the fixture is still code-free (find
#   .../fake_site/vendor -name '*.php') rather than an actual vendored Drupal.
CVE-2024-55637

# why: same package_manager fixture, same empty vendor/drupal/core. No code.
# until: 2027-02-01 -- recheck as above.
CVE-2024-55638

# why: same package_manager fixture, same empty vendor/drupal/core. The SQL
#   injection is in Drupal core database code that is not present at 9.8.0 in
#   this image at all; the running Drupal is 11.4.4, which trivy scans
#   separately and reports clean for this ID.
# until: 2027-02-01 -- recheck as above, and re-confirm the real
#   web/core/composer.json version is a patched release.
CVE-2026-9082

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Joomla's own vendor tree (joomla flavors)
#
# build/site/web/libraries/vendor is the CMS's composer tree, shipped by the
# Joomla 4.4.14 release civibuild downloads — not CiviCRM's and not ours. The
# versions are pinned in Joomla's composer.lock; raising them means shipping a
# Joomla nobody else runs, and the point of this flavor is to test CiviCRM
# against the Joomla people actually have.
#
# Separately worth knowing: Joomla 4.4 is past upstream's support window. That
# is a question about which Joomla civibuild's site type installs, not a
# question this file can answer.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# why: laminas-diactoros 2.4.1 in Joomla's vendor tree. The multiline-header
#   termination bug needs an application that builds PSR-7 messages from
#   attacker-controlled header names/values; the throwaway Joomla site these
#   images bake serves CiviCRM on localhost and does not. Pinned by Joomla's
#   own composer.lock.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after the next Joomla release civibuild picks
#   up; drop the entry if the tree has moved.
CVE-2023-29530

# why: phpseclib 3.0.37 in Joomla's vendor tree. The AES-CBC padding-oracle
#   needs an attacker who can submit chosen ciphertexts to a decrypt endpoint
#   and time the responses; this is a single-user localhost dev site with no
#   such endpoint exposed. Pinned by Joomla's composer.lock.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after the next Joomla release.
CVE-2026-32935

# why: phpseclib 3.0.37, same tree. Requires loading untrusted ASN.1/X.509
#   input; nothing in the baked site parses attacker-supplied certificates.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after the next Joomla release.
CVE-2026-44167

# why: symfony/yaml 5.4.23 in Joomla's vendor tree. Unbounded recursion needs
#   attacker-controlled YAML; the YAML Joomla parses is its own configuration
#   and language files, which ship with the release. Pinned by Joomla's
#   composer.lock.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after the next Joomla release.
CVE-2026-45133

# why: symfony/yaml 5.4.23, same tree — alias expansion memory exhaustion,
#   same "only parses its own config" argument.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after the next Joomla release.
CVE-2026-45304

# why: symfony/yaml 5.4.23, same tree — ReDoS in Parser::cleanup(), same
#   "only parses its own config" argument.
# until: 2026-11-01 -- recheck after the next Joomla release.
CVE-2026-45305
